A rare condition affects $0.1%$ of the population: $PP(A) = 0.001$. A test has true positive rate $PP(P mid(|) A) = 0.98$ and false positive rate $PP(P mid(|) A^(cp)) = 0.01$. Testing positive,
$
  PP(A mid(|) P) & = (PP(P mid(|) A) PP(A)) / (PP(P mid(|) A) PP(A) + PP(P mid(|) A^(cp)) PP(A^(cp))) \
                 & = (0.98 times 0.001)/(0.98 times 0.001 + 0.01 times 0.999) approx 0.09.
$
Because the condition is rare, the posterior probability stays small even though the test is highly reliable.
